1
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:07,720
CompanyOs gives a business a place to define
its purpose,

2
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:10,620
organise its agents, and supervise their work.

3
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:13,300
Let's explore it through Northstar Care,

4
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:18,120
a customer-care agency serving our fictional
insurance client, Harbor Mutual.

5
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:22,320
Starting the demo creates a fresh company with
a prepared brief.

6
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:24,780
Here we can review who it serves,

7
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:28,340
the outcome we want, and its first objective.

8
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:31,140
The team has distinct responsibilities.

9
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:35,060
The manager coordinates work, the backlog
tracks gaps,

10
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:38,400
and the policy and claims guides handle
customer questions.

11
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,620
Each profile has instructions we can inspect
and edit.

12
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:47,320
Company knowledge gives the team a shared,
versioned source of facts.

13
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:50,660
Operating limits define the boundaries around
its work.

14
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:04,200
On the marketplace, the agency's policy and
claims

15
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:07,300
guides are published as customer-facing
services.

16
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:11,680
A customer can discover a service and open a
conversation directly.

17
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,480
These listings belong to the company we just
created.

18
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,240
Let's ask about the collision deductible.

19
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:23,960
The guide retrieves the approved policy
wording and includes its source revision.

20
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:27,720
This local demo uses exact document lookup,

21
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:30,140
so we can see where the answer came from.

22
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:35,500
Customer payments gives the owner a place to
inspect

23
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:38,240
received-payment receipts and their
references.

24
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:41,760
This new demo company has no payments yet.

25
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:46,740
Below it is a separately labelled receipt from
a prior mainnet service sale

26
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:51,740
of ten USDC cents, with links to its
settlement and ledger record.

27
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:55,400
Now let's inspect a recorded live recovery
run.

28
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:59,540
A customer asked which address was needed to
complete a claims intake.

29
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:02,460
The support agent's runbook was missing that
guidance,

30
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:04,460
so the first attempt escalated.

31
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,260
The gap reached the backlog, which delegated
repairs.

32
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:11,600
The knowledge curator brought the exact
requirement in from

33
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:15,260
an owner-approved source and saved a new
runbook revision.

34
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:18,400
The template developer added the missing
address field.

35
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:23,140
The same fixed tests rejected the old template
and accepted the repaired version.

36
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:54,480
The support agent then retried the same
customer

37
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:56,920
request with the updated knowledge and
template.

38
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:00,900
It recorded the customer-supplied mailing
address and produced a complete intake.

39
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:04,880
The result is a lasting change to how this
organisation handles

40
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:09,500
the next request: a gap became work, the work
produced a checked repair,

41
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,120
and the repair reached the customer-facing
task.
